<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Instructions of Shuruppak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2600–2500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Instructions of Shuruppak is a Sumerian wisdom text in which Shuruppak, a ruler of the city of that name, gives proverbial advice to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Ziusudra</a>, the flood hero. Its earliest copies, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>, date to about 2600–2500 BCE, making it one of the oldest surviving works of literature. Its counsel covers farming, property, speech and conduct.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak.jpg" alt="Instructions of Shuruppak" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Instructions_of_Shurrupak,_Sumerian_proverb_collection,_c._2400_BC_-_Oriental_Institute_Museum,_University_of_Chicago_-_DSC07114.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
